find - search files by their name
grep - search inside for text
        grep "<text>" <file>
& - Run the command but do not prevent further work before
    it finished. Runs in background
&& - Runs multiple commands sequentially
        <command 1> && <command 2>
> - Redirect output and send it to a file, overwriting the
    file
>> - Redirector does same thing but appends it to the bottom
    of the file
touch - creates a file
mkdir - creates a directory
cp - copy file/folder
mv - move file/folder
rm - remove file/folder
wget - allows you to download files from the web onto the
    local machine
    EX:
    wget https://assets.tryhackme.com/additional/myfile.txt
    This downloads a file called myfile.txt to the local
    machine from the assets.tryhackme.com website
SCP - copy files between systems using SSH for encryption
    and authentication
    EX:
    Remote IP - 192.168.1.30
    remote name - ubuntu
    name of file on remote system - documents
    name we wish to store it as on our system - notes.txt
    command:
     scp ubuntu@192.168.1.30:/home/ubuntu/documents.txt notes.txt
    
    now flip it - storing a file from our system on a remote
     scp notes.txt ubuntu@192.168.1.30:/home/ubuntu/documents.txt

python3 offers http.server automatically
    python3 -m http.server
        will automatically start the server on port 8000
you can then use wget to retrieve files being served by python
    *Note you'll need to specify port 8000 along with the
     serving IP addr
ps - displays running processes
    to see processes run by other users and those run not from
     a session, we need to use the aux flag: ps aux
top - pretty much the same as ps but gives running and updating
    analysis rather than a static, point-in-time snapshot
kill - kills the process associated with the specified PID
SIGTERM - Kill the process but let it cleanup first
SIGKILL - kill the process, don't let it clean up
SIGSTOP - Stop/Suspend a process

systemctl - allows us to interact with systemd to mess with 
    daemons
    provides 5 options:
    start, stop, enable, disable, status
    systemctl <option> <service/daemon>

pressing CTRL+Z is a way of 'backgrounding' an operation
    think of it like the '&' operator but in the moment
use 'fg' to bring it back to the foreground to see it
    running